## Properties, not public fields

```csharp
public class Product
{
    private decimal _price;                       // a field: storage
    public decimal Price                          // a property: controlled access
    {
        get => _price;
        set => _price = value >= 0 ? value : throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(value));
    }
    public string Name { get; set; } = "";        // auto-property with an initialiser
    public string Sku { get; init; } = "";        // settable only during initialisation
    public required string Category { get; init; } // must be set by the creator (C# 11)
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; } = DateTime.UtcNow; // get-only: set here or in the constructor
}
```

Properties let you add validation or change the storage later without changing callers. Public fields are only appropriate for small structs and constants.

An auto-property such as `Name { get; set; }` compiles to a hidden field and two small methods, which the JIT inlines, so it costs nothing at runtime. The difference is the contract: an interface can declare a property but not a field, and turning a public field into a property later is a breaking change for assemblies already compiled against it.

## Constructors

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IOrderRepository _repo;
    public OrderService(IOrderRepository repo) => _repo = repo;
}

// C# 12 primary constructor: the parameter is in scope for the whole class
public class OrderService(IOrderRepository repo)
{
    public Task<Order?> Find(int id) => repo.FindAsync(id);
}
```

`readonly` fields can be assigned only in the declaration or a constructor. Primary-constructor parameters are captured, mutable variables, not readonly fields; assign them to a `readonly` field if that matters. A method that writes `repo = other;` compiles, and every later call then uses the new value. A record is different: there a positional parameter becomes a public init-only property.

## static

Static members belong to the type, not to instances: `Counter.Count`. Static state is shared by every request in a web app, so mutable statics are a concurrency and test-isolation hazard; keep statics for pure helpers, constants and factory methods. The classic case is a static `List<Order>` used as a cache: two requests calling `Add` at the same moment can corrupt the list, and a test that fills it leaks orders into the next test. Register a singleton service holding a `ConcurrentDictionary` instead.

## Records

```csharp
public record Money(decimal Amount, string Currency);

var a = new Money(1m, "EUR");
var b = new Money(1m, "EUR");
a == b                     // true: value equality over all members
a.ToString()               // "Money { Amount = 1, Currency = EUR }"
var c = a with { Amount = 2m };   // non-destructive copy with one change
var (amount, currency) = a;       // positional records deconstruct
```

A positional record gets init-only properties, value-based `Equals` / `GetHashCode` / `==`, a readable `ToString`, `Deconstruct` and `with` support. You can add members and validation to a record body.

Equality compares every member with that member's own `Equals`, so a record holding a `List<string>` compares the list by reference: two records with lists of equal contents are not equal. A record body may also redeclare a positional member as a settable property. That compiles, but changing a record after it went into a `HashSet` changes its hash code, and the set no longer finds it.

## class, record or struct?

| type | equality | typical use |
|---|---|---|
| `class` | reference | entities with identity and lifecycle (an Order you load and change), services |
| `record` (class) | value | DTOs, commands, events, value objects (Money, Address), API results |
| `struct` / `record struct` | value, copied | tiny immutable values (coordinates, ids) where allocation matters |

Two `Customer` class instances with the same fields are NOT `==`; two equal records are. Do not use records for EF Core entities that are tracked and mutated: their value equality and immutability fight the change tracker (topic 19).

## Encapsulation for domain objects

Expose behaviour, not setters: `order.AddLine(sku, qty)` can enforce "no more than 50 lines" and "cannot change a shipped order"; a public `List<Line> Lines { get; set; }` cannot. Expose collections as `IReadOnlyList<T>`.

A common shape keeps `private readonly List<Line> _lines = new();` and exposes `public IReadOnlyList<Line> Lines => _lines;`. `AddLine` checks the count and the order's status before it adds, so callers can read and loop but cannot change the lines without the checks. A caller can still cast the view back to `List<Line>`; return `_lines.AsReadOnly()` when that matters.

## Delegates are typed function values

```csharp
Func<int, int> square = x => x * x;        // takes an int, returns an int
Action<string> log = msg => Console.WriteLine(msg);  // returns nothing
Predicate<Order> isPaid = o => o.Status == "paid";   // same as Func<Order, bool>
square(4)   // 16
```

LINQ, `List.RemoveAll`, `Task.Run`, minimal API handlers and middleware are all delegates. `Func<..., TResult>` returns a value; `Action<...>` does not.

A delegate instance holds a method and, for an instance method or a capturing lambda, the target object it runs on. Delegate types with the same signature still do not convert into each other: a `Predicate<Order>` variable cannot be passed where a `Func<Order, bool>` is expected, although the same lambda converts to either type.

## Closures and the capture trap

A lambda captures **variables**, not values. In a `for` loop there is ONE loop variable:

```csharp
var fs = new List<Func<int>>();
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) fs.Add(() => i);
fs.Select(f => f())          // 3, 3, 3

foreach (var x in new[] { 0, 1, 2 }) fs2.Add(() => x);   // 0, 1, 2: foreach gets a fresh variable per iteration (since C# 5)
```

Fix the `for` case by copying into a local inside the loop: `var copy = i; fs.Add(() => copy);`. Captured variables also keep objects alive as long as the delegate lives. Each lambda also becomes its own compiler-generated method, so two lambdas with the same text are two different methods, which matters as soon as you try to remove one with `-=`.

## Multicast delegates

`+=` combines delegates; invoking runs them all, in order:

```csharp
Action<string> a = s => log.Add("A" + s);
a += s => log.Add("B" + s);
a("x");                       // A then B
Func<int> f = () => 1; f += () => 2;
f()                           // 2: only the LAST return value comes back
```

Combining builds a new delegate with a longer invocation list. Delegates are immutable, so `+=` on a variable swaps in that new instance. If one target throws, the targets after it do not run and the exception reaches the caller. `GetInvocationList()` returns the targets as an array, which is how you call each one inside its own `try`, or collect every return value.

## Events

```csharp
public class Publisher
{
    public event EventHandler<int>? Changed;
    public void Raise(int n) => Changed?.Invoke(this, n);   // ?. : safe with no subscribers
}
pub.Changed += OnChanged;
pub.Changed -= OnChanged;
```

An `event` is a delegate field that outsiders can only `+=` and `-=`: they cannot invoke it or overwrite the list. Handlers run **synchronously** on the raising thread, in subscription order; an exception in one stops the rest.

Inside the declaring class the event behaves like the delegate field it wraps: the class can invoke it, assign it with `=` and read its invocation list. `Changed?.Invoke(this, n)` reads the field once, so a handler removed on another thread cannot turn the call into a `NullReferenceException` between the check and the call.

## Unsubscribe, or leak

The publisher's delegate list holds a reference to every subscriber. A long-lived publisher (a static cache, a singleton service) keeps short-lived subscribers (pages, view models, per-request objects) alive forever unless they unsubscribe.

`-=` only removes a handler that is **the same delegate**: `p.Changed -= (_, x) => n += x;` creates a NEW lambda and removes nothing. Keep a reference to the handler, or return a subscription object:

```csharp
public IDisposable Subscribe(Action<decimal> handler)
{
    EventHandler<decimal> h = (_, p) => handler(p);
    PriceChanged += h;
    return new Subscription(() => PriceChanged -= h);
}
using var sub = feed.Subscribe(p => Console.WriteLine(p));   // disposed: unsubscribed
```

This is the pattern behind `IObservable`, `IOptionsMonitor.OnChange` and many client libraries.

## Throwing and catching

```csharp
try
{
    var order = repo.Load(id);
}
catch (TimeoutException ex)                     // most specific first
{
    throw new RepositoryUnavailableException("orders store unavailable", ex);   // wrap, keep the cause
}
catch (HttpRequestException ex) when (ex.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.NotFound)  // exception filter
{
    return null;
}
finally
{
    metrics.Stop();                              // runs on success, on exception and on return
}
```

- Catch clauses are tried top to bottom; putting a base type (`Exception`) before a derived one is a compile error (CS0160).
- An **exception filter** (`when (...)`) decides whether to catch at all; if it is false, the exception keeps propagating with its stack untouched.
- `finally` always runs (except for process-killing failures): use it, or `using`, for cleanup.

Because a filter runs before the stack unwinds, a filter that logs and returns false records the exception with all frames still live and then lets it pass on untouched. A filter that throws is treated as false.

## throw versus throw ex

```csharp
catch (Exception ex) { log.Error(ex); throw; }      // rethrows, keeping the ORIGINAL stack trace
catch (Exception ex) { log.Error(ex); throw ex; }   // resets the stack trace to this line: the origin is lost
```

Always `throw;` to rethrow. To add context, wrap: `throw new XException("context", ex)`; the original is kept as `InnerException` and logged with it.

The difference shows in production logs. With `throw ex;` the trace of a NullReferenceException deep in a repository starts at the controller's catch block, and nobody can tell which line failed. Code analysis flags it as CA2200. To rethrow later, outside the catch block, for example after collecting the exception from another thread, `ExceptionDispatchInfo.Capture(ex).Throw()` keeps the original frames in the trace.

## Custom exceptions

```csharp
public class NotFoundException(string entity, object id)
    : Exception($"{entity} {id} was not found");
```

Create them for failures callers want to handle differently (not found, conflict, domain rule violated). Do not use exceptions for normal control flow (a missing optional value, a failed validation in a loop): they are expensive and hide the happy path. `TryParse`-style methods and result types are cheaper for expected outcomes. A throw captures a stack trace and unwinds frames; a `TryParse` that fails just returns false. Parsing a 10,000-line import that has one bad value per line costs thousands of throws with `int.Parse` and none with `int.TryParse`.

## IDisposable and using

Objects holding non-memory resources (files, sockets, database connections, timers) implement `IDisposable`. `using` guarantees `Dispose` even when an exception is thrown:

```csharp
using var conn = new SqlConnection(cs);      // using DECLARATION: disposed at the end of the enclosing scope
using var cmd = conn.CreateCommand();
// ...
// at scope exit: cmd is disposed first, then conn (reverse order of declaration)
```

The block form `using (var r = ...) { }` disposes at the closing brace. Async resources implement `IAsyncDisposable`: `await using`.

What the garbage collector does NOT do: close files or return connections to the pool at a predictable time. Forgetting `Dispose` on HttpClient-like or connection-like objects exhausts sockets or the connection pool under load. (Classes owned by the DI container, topic 15, are disposed by the container: do not dispose injected services yourself.)

## Where to catch

Catch where you can **do something**: retry, translate to a domain error, return a fallback, or add context. Everywhere else, let exceptions propagate to one global handler (ASP.NET Core's exception handler middleware, topic 17) that logs them and returns a 500 problem response. Never write `catch (Exception) { }`: a swallowed exception turns a crash into silent data corruption.

A concrete case: a payment client may retry a `TimeoutException` once, because a second attempt can succeed. A `NullReferenceException` from the same call is a bug; retrying it or catching it changes nothing, so it belongs to the global handler and the logs.

## A pipeline of nested delegates

Each middleware receives the `HttpContext` and a `next` delegate. Code before `await next(context)` runs on the way IN, code after it runs on the way OUT:

```csharp
app.Use(async (ctx, next) =>
{
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();          // in
    await next(ctx);
    log.LogInformation("{Path} took {Ms} ms", ctx.Request.Path, sw.ElapsedMilliseconds);  // out
});
```

With middleware A registered before B, a request flows **A in → B in → endpoint → B out → A out**. The pipeline is built once at startup, in registration order: the first registered is the OUTERMOST.

## Short-circuiting and terminal middleware

A middleware that does not call `next` ends the request there: a health check, a rate limiter answering 429, an auth check answering 401, static files that served a file. `app.Run(...)` registers a terminal middleware that never calls next. A short-circuit is an answer, not an error: the outer middleware still run their after-next code, so a request logger records the 429 like any other response.

## Order matters

The template order is not arbitrary:

```csharp
app.UseExceptionHandler();      // 1. outermost: catches exceptions from everything after it
app.UseHsts(); app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseStaticFiles();           // serve files early, skipping auth for them
app.UseRouting();               // pick the endpoint
app.UseCors();
app.UseAuthentication();        // who are you? (sets HttpContext.User)
app.UseAuthorization();         // may you call THIS endpoint? (needs both routing and the user)
app.MapControllers(); app.MapGet(...);   // endpoints
```

- The exception handler must come first, or exceptions thrown by middleware registered before it are not caught.
- Authorization needs the endpoint (from routing) and the user (from authentication), so it comes after both.
- Middleware that should see only matched endpoints goes after routing.

## Exception handling

`app.UseExceptionHandler()` together with `builder.Services.AddProblemDetails()` turns any unhandled exception into a **500 problem-details** response without the exception message, and logs the exception. In .NET 8+ you can register `IExceptionHandler` implementations to map specific exceptions (a `NotFoundException` to 404) in one place. The developer exception page (with stack traces) is for Development only.

## Writing a middleware class

```csharp
public class CorrelationIdMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
{
    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext ctx)
    {
        var id = ctx.Request.Headers["X-Correlation-Id"].FirstOrDefault() ?? Guid.NewGuid().ToString();
        ctx.Response.Headers["X-Correlation-Id"] = id;
        await next(ctx);
    }
}
app.UseMiddleware<CorrelationIdMiddleware>();
```

Conventional middleware is created ONCE (a singleton): inject scoped services into `InvokeAsync` parameters, not into the constructor.

`UseMiddleware<T>` fills the constructor (the `RequestDelegate` plus singleton services) when the pipeline is built, and resolves the extra `InvokeAsync` parameters from the request's services on each call, as in `InvokeAsync(HttpContext ctx, AppDb db)`. A class that implements `IMiddleware` works differently: it is resolved from DI on each request, so it must be registered, for example with `AddScoped<T>()`.

## The response has started

Headers and the status code are sent when the body starts. After that, changing `StatusCode` or headers throws (`Response.HasStarted` tells you). Set headers before calling next, or register `ctx.Response.OnStarting(...)` callbacks.

A handler that writes part of a 200 body and then throws leaves the exception handler nothing to fix: the client already has the status line, so the handler rethrows and the client sees a cut-off response. `OnStarting` callbacks run just before the headers go out, the last moment to add one.

## Validate at the edge

Every request body is untrusted. Validate shape and simple rules where the request enters, before any business logic runs, and answer **400** with field-level errors.

```csharp
public class SignupDto
{
    [Required, EmailAddress] public string? Email { get; set; }
    [Required, StringLength(50, MinimumLength = 1)] public string? Name { get; set; }
    [Range(13, 120)] public int Age { get; set; }
}
```

`[Range(min, max)]` is inclusive at both ends: `[Range(1, 5)]` accepts 1 and 5 and rejects 0 and 6.

## Automatic validation

- **Controllers** with `[ApiController]`: an invalid model never reaches the action; the framework returns 400 with a **ValidationProblemDetails** body.
- **Minimal APIs** in .NET 10: call `builder.Services.AddValidation()` and parameters with DataAnnotations are validated the same way. (Earlier versions needed endpoint filters or a library such as FluentValidation.)

The response body looks like this:

```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.1",
  "title": "One or more validation errors occurred.",
  "status": 400,
  "errors": { "Email": ["The Email field is required."], "Age": ["The field Age must be between 13 and 120."] },
  "traceId": "00-..."
}
```

Clients can map `errors` straight onto form fields.

## Problem details (RFC 9457)

`{ type, title, status, detail, instance }` plus extensions is the standard error shape for HTTP APIs. `builder.Services.AddProblemDetails()` makes the framework use it for errors (exceptions, status-code pages), and `Results.Problem(...)` / `Results.ValidationProblem(errors)` produce it from your own code. One consistent error shape for the whole API is worth more than clever per-endpoint messages.

## DTOs against over-posting

Never bind request bodies straight to entities:

```csharp
app.MapPut("/users/{id:int}", async (int id, UpdateUserDto dto, AppDb db) => {
    var user = await db.Users.FindAsync(id);
    if (user is null) return Results.NotFound();
    user.Name = dto.Name;        // copy ONLY what the client may change
    user.Bio = dto.Bio;
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return Results.NoContent();
});
```

Binding to the entity would let a client send `"isAdmin": true` or `"balance": 1000000` (**over-posting** / mass assignment). Separate input DTOs, output DTOs (no password hashes) and entities. The output side matters as much: returning the entity serialises every property, `PasswordHash` included, while a response record lists only what the client may see.

## Shape validation versus business rules

DataAnnotations check a request in isolation: required, lengths, ranges, formats. Rules that need data (the email is already taken, the product is out of stock, the order is already shipped) belong in the service, and usually map to **409 Conflict** or **422 Unprocessable Content** rather than 400. Keep both kinds returning problem details. A unique index in the database stays as the backstop: two signups racing with one email can both pass the service check, the index rejects the second insert, and the service translates that violation into the same 409.

## Normalise as you validate

Trim strings, normalise case for emails and codes, treat empty strings as missing where the field is optional. Doing it once at the boundary keeps the rest of the code simple and the database clean. For example, `" Ana@Example.com "` becomes `ana@example.com` before the uniqueness check, so one person cannot register twice with different spacing or capitals. Leave alone what carries meaning: a password must stay exactly as typed.

## Seams: inject what the test must control

A handler that calls `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`, `Guid.NewGuid()` or a real HTTP client has hidden inputs, so a test cannot pin its output and ends up asserting "roughly a week from now". Take those inputs as dependencies instead: `TimeProvider` (the base library's clock abstraction) for time, an id generator, and an interface such as `IEmailSender` or `IWebhookSender` for every call that leaves the process. Production registers `TimeProvider.System`; a test passes a clock it can set and advance.

```csharp
public sealed class InviteService(TimeProvider clock, IInviteStore store)
{
    public Invite Create(string email)
    {
        var now = clock.GetUtcNow();                 // read once per operation
        return store.Add(new Invite(email, now, now.AddDays(7)));
    }
}
```

Read the clock once per operation, so `CreatedAt` and `ExpiresAt` come from the same instant.

## Fakes, stubs and mocks

A **stub** returns canned answers. A **fake** is a small working implementation: an in-memory store that assigns ids and refuses a duplicate email, a clock you can advance, a sender that keeps an outbox. A **mock** records calls so the test can assert on them. Prefer fakes for stores, clocks and senders: the test then checks outcomes, and a refactor that keeps the behaviour keeps the tests green. Assert on an interaction only when the interaction is the behaviour ("exactly one webhook was sent").

A fake must keep the rules of the thing it replaces. A dictionary-backed store that accepts `Ana@x.io` and `ana@x.io` lets the tests pass while production returns 500 when the unique index fires.

## WebApplicationFactory runs the real pipeline in memory

`WebApplicationFactory<Program>` (package `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing`) boots the app's real `Program` on an in-memory `TestServer`. `factory.CreateClient()` returns an `HttpClient` whose requests never open a socket, yet still run routing, middleware, model binding, validation, authentication, authorization, the endpoint and JSON serialization. That is the right level for "POST /invites returns 201 with a Location header" or "an anonymous DELETE gets 401".

```csharp
public sealed class ApiFactory : WebApplicationFactory<Program>
{
    public FakeEmailSender Email { get; } = new();
    public ManualClock Clock { get; } = new(DateTimeOffset.Parse("2026-03-01T09:00:00Z"));

    protected override void ConfigureWebHost(IWebHostBuilder builder)
    {
        builder.UseEnvironment("Testing");
        builder.ConfigureTestServices(services =>
        {
            services.RemoveAll<IEmailSender>();
            services.AddSingleton<IEmailSender>(Email);
            services.RemoveAll<TimeProvider>();
            services.AddSingleton<TimeProvider>(Clock);
        });
    }
}
```

## Swap only the edges

`ConfigureTestServices` runs after the app's own registrations. When a constructor asks for one `IEmailSender`, the container hands it the LAST registration, so an added fake wins there; but anything that takes `IEnumerable<IEmailSender>` still receives the real sender as well. Call `RemoveAll` before adding the fake. A `TryAdd...` call in the override changes nothing when the app already registered the service. Keep the real routing, validation, serialization and queries; replace only what leaves the process (email, payments, webhooks) and what the test must control (the clock).

## A real database, isolated per test

EF Core's InMemory provider is not a relational database: it runs no SQL and does not enforce relational constraints such as unique indexes, so it can pass a test that the real database would fail. Use the real engine instead, with the production schema: a plain unique index compares text case-sensitively in SQLite and PostgreSQL alike, so `Ana@x.io` and `ana@x.io` only collide when the column is declared `COLLATE NOCASE` (SQLite) or the unique index is on `lower(email)`. A SQLite `:memory:` database lives only as long as its connection, so keep one connection open for the factory's lifetime and create the schema at startup; or start a throwaway PostgreSQL container. Give each test class its own database, or reset the rows each test touches: tests that lean on each other's rows pass alone and fail in another order.

## Authenticate as a test user

Register a test authentication handler that builds the `ClaimsPrincipal` from a request header (for example `X-Test-User` and `X-Test-Role`), so the real authorization rules still run. Then assert at the HTTP level: anonymous gets 401, the wrong role gets 403, the owner gets 200. Check status codes, headers such as `Location`, the ProblemDetails body and what the fakes recorded, not the private methods behind the endpoint.

## A worker is a loop over a stopping token

A `BackgroundService` overrides one method, `ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)`. The host starts it with the app and, on shutdown, cancels `stoppingToken` and waits for `ExecuteAsync` to return, but only up to `HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout`, 30 seconds by default. A worker is a singleton, so it creates a scope per job for scoped services such as a `DbContext`:

```csharp
public sealed class ReceiptWorker(ChannelReader<ReceiptJob> jobs, IServiceScopeFactory scopes, ILogger<ReceiptWorker> logger) : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await foreach (var job in jobs.ReadAllAsync(stoppingToken))
        {
            try
            {
                using var scope = scopes.CreateScope();
                await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IReceiptSender>().SendAsync(job, stoppingToken);
            }
            catch (Exception e) when (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                logger.LogError(e, "Receipt job {JobId} failed", job.Id);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## A bounded queue between requests and the worker

`System.Threading.Channels` gives a thread-safe queue with async waits. Bound it: `Channel.CreateBounded<ReceiptJob>(new BoundedChannelOptions(100) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait })`. When 100 jobs are waiting, `WriteAsync` waits for the worker to free a slot, and `TryWrite` returns `false` at once, so an endpoint can answer 503 instead of holding the request. That is backpressure. An unbounded channel never pushes back: a slow worker turns into a memory leak. The other full modes drop an item (`DropOldest`, `DropNewest`, `DropWrite`), which suits telemetry, not orders.

## One bad item must not stop the loop

Wrap each job, not the loop, in `try`/`catch`: log the failure with the job's id and move on. Since .NET 8 an exception that escapes `ExecuteAsync` stops the whole host, because `HostOptions.BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior` defaults to `StopHost`. With `Ignore` the app keeps running with a dead worker, which is worse: nothing processes the queue and only one log line says why. Filter on the stopping token rather than on the exception type: a job's own timeout throws `OperationCanceledException` too, and it is a failed job, not a reason to stop.

## Graceful stop: stop taking, drain, hand back

On shutdown, first refuse new jobs: `writer.Complete()` makes `TryWrite` return `false`, while the items already queued stay readable, and a reader's `ReadAllAsync` loop yields those jobs and then ends. Then finish what you can before the deadline (the token that `StopAsync` receives fires at the shutdown timeout). Whatever has not finished by then, the job in flight and the ones still queued, goes back to durable storage or a retry queue. An in-memory channel dies with the process, so work that must happen (a payment, an email to a customer) belongs in an outbox table or a durable queue, with the channel only as a fast path.

## Periodic work with PeriodicTimer

```csharp
using var timer = new PeriodicTimer(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(15));
while (await timer.WaitForNextTickAsync(stoppingToken))
{
    await SweepAsync(stoppingToken);
}
```

`WaitForNextTickAsync` throws `OperationCanceledException` when the token is cancelled and returns `false` once the timer is disposed. Runs never overlap, because the next wait starts only after the work. If one run takes 25 seconds with a 10-second period, the ticks missed meanwhile collapse into one: the next wait returns at once, and later ticks keep the original 10-second rhythm.

## No async void, no fire-and-forget from a request

An exception in an `async void` method has no `Task` to land on, so it is rethrown on the thread pool and terminates the process. Use `async Task` and await it. Starting work from an endpoint with `_ = Task.Run(...)` has the same blind spot plus two more: the request's scoped `DbContext` is disposed when the response is sent, and the host does not know about the task, so shutdown kills it halfway. Write a job to the channel instead and let the worker own it.
